  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory has been issued for Wednesday \n11 AM until 8 PM. Anomalously hot temperatures are expected to \npersist into Friday.\n\nA deep subtropical ridge of around 596 dam sliding east into \nthe TN Valley in the 12z H5 RAOBs this morning is forecast to \nwobble gradually eastward and stretch over the Carolinas by \nThurs morning. Highly anomalous H850 temperatures (at-or-above \nthe 99.5 percentile) initially directed over the Ohio Valley and\nLower Great Lakes today, will gradually warm to around 20-22C \nand slowly be directed over the southern Mid-Atlantic Wed \nthrough Fri. This pattern is highly favorable for an early \nSeptember heat wave with minimal convective chances due to \ninfluence from the strong subtropical ridge. \n\nLow-lvl thicknesses and diabatic techniques line up with \nstatistical guidance and supports widespread mid/upper 90s \ncontinuing through Fri. A few locations may reach triple digit \nair temperatures, but widespread greater than 100 degrees is not\nexpected. Heat index values south and east of Raleigh are \nlikely to reach if not eclipse 105 degrees for several hours due\nto better low-lvl moisture in place. Additively, weak surface \nwinds and abundant sunshine will compound the risk for heat \nrelated illnesses, especially individuals sensitive to the heat \nor partake in strenuous outdoor activities in the afternoon \nduring peak heating. Anyone without effective overnight cooling \nis at greater risk for heat illness as several days with \nanomalously warm overnight low in the upper 70s will provide \nminimal relief from hot afternoon temperatures and humid \nconditions. As such, a Heat Advisory has been issued for Wed (11\nAM until 8 PM). This is only the 3rd Heat Advisory issued in \nthe month of September by the Raleigh office and the first since\n2020. Additional Heat Advisories will likely be needed through \nFri. \n\nIncreasing convective chances, an approaching cold front and \nnortheast flow in its wake should put an end to the dangerous \nheat by Sun.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Labor Day weekend through Monday gradually \ntrends cooler along with highest thunderstorm chances Friday \nand Saturday. \n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance continues to show a gradual\nwestward shift of the anomalously strong upper-ridge. Come \nThursday, it is directly overhead over the southern \nAppalachians, but sliding west toward the Central and Southern \nPlains this weekend and early next week. We continue to watch as\nthat happens a more northwesterly flow developing, especially \nFriday onward. This pattern shift continues to support a cold \nfront approaching late Friday but especially Saturday, before \nmoving through for Sunday. \n\nThe best chance of showers and thunderstorms is favored late \nFriday and Saturday during peak afternoon and evening hours, \npre-frontal and along the front. Given the atmospheric setup, \nconditions appear favorable for locally damaging winds. SPC has \nintroduced a day 4 Slight Risk across our NE counties Friday. \nAdd to that, ML/AI guidance also favors a severe risk on \nSaturday, though ensembles and deterministic solutions differ on\nthe frontal position and speed. Something that is also new is \nthat SPC did include a very small area of a Marginal Risk \nThursday evening across our northern Coastal Plain counties. \nSome deterministic scenarios and nearly half of the EPS members \nshow a mid-level disturbance riding the ridge to favor some low-\nend storm chances in our NE Thursday evening.\n\nThere is a small but noticeable signal that once the front \nmoves through, we may not cool off as much as originally \nadvertised. Some ensemble and deterministic solutions hang the \nfront just to our south and west into early next week, resulting\nin highs in the middle to upper 80s and perhaps not as less \nhumid. How that will trend in the coming days remains to be \nseen, but nevertheless it should be drier with just isolated \nafternoon storms through the Labor Day holiday.",
